<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sidoarjo</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sidoarjo is a town and regency in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-java">East Java</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a>, on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brantas-river">Brantas</a> delta just south of Surabaya. The fertile delta was the heartland of the 11th-century kingdom of Janggala. Today the area is an industrial and fishpond district, known also for the Lusi mud volcano, which erupted in 2006 and buried several villages.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sidoarjo.jpg" alt="Sidoarjo" width="320" height="171" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: wisatasidoarjo.com · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Alun-alun-sidoarjo.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Sidoarjo Regency covers about 719.34 km2, the smallest regency of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-java">East Java</a>, and borders Surabaya, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gresik">Gresik</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pasuruan">Pasuruan</a>, Mojokerto and the Madura Strait. Its northern districts, such as Waru and Taman, adjoin Surabaya and are the most densely settled. Coastal districts such as Sedati and Porong lie between 0 and 3 metres above sea level and are given over to shrimp and milkfish ponds, which earned the regency the nicknames Delta City and Shrimp City. The climate is tropical savanna, with heavy rain from December to April.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The area belonged to the Kahuripan kingdom founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/airlangga">Airlangga</a> around 1019, with its capital at the mouth of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brantas-river">Brantas River</a>, before the kingdom was divided into Janggala and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kediri-kingdom">Kediri</a> in 1045. Janggala's capital lay at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hujung-galuh">Hujung Galuh</a>, partly within present-day Sidoarjo and Surabaya, which grew into a major port of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/majapahit-empire">Majapahit Empire</a> in the 14th century. The red-brick Pari Temple, built around 1371 in the reign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hayam-wuruk">Hayam Wuruk</a>, and Dermo Temple, believed to date from the mid-14th century, survive from that age. In the colonial period the district was known for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugarcane">sugarcane</a> and had 10 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> mills, the oldest, Watu Tulis, built in 1838.</p><p>From May 2006 hot mud flowed from a natural gas well drilled by Lapindo Brantas in Porong District, displacing more than 10,000 people. The flow began on 28 May, when the drill penetrated a layer of liquid sediment, and attempts to pump concrete into the well failed. Some scientists suggested that the Yogyakarta earthquake of the day before had opened paths for the mud; others blamed the drilling. The daily flow grew from some 50,000 cubic metres in August to some 125,000 in September, and on 26 September barriers failed and more villages were flooded.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The regency has its capital in Sidoarjo District and is divided into 18 districts. With an estimated population of about two million in 2025, it is part of the Gerbangkertosusila metropolitan area around Surabaya. Juanda International Airport lies in Sedati, and manufacturing accounts for nearly 40% of the economy.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sidoarjo Regency” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Janggala arose around 1045 from Airlangga's partition of Kahuripan, which gave one son the eastern lands around the Brantas delta, the old capital Kahuripan and the coastal ports near modern Surabaya and <b>Sidoarjo</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/janggala">Janggala</a></li></ul></section>
